Day 305/350: Matrix Similarity After Cyclic Shifts

Probelm Statement #2946

Problem Statement Explantion:

You are given a 0-indexed m×n integer matrix mat and an integer k.

Each row is shifted k times:
Even-indexed rows (0, 2, 4...) are shifted to the left.
Odd-indexed rows (1, 3, 5...) are shifted to the right.
Return true if the matrix and the modified matrix are identical after all shifts, and false otherwise.

Solution Approach:

The core observation is that shifting a row cyclically and expecting it to remain identical is equivalent to checking if the row has a repeating pattern that aligns with the shift amount k.

Simplify k: Since shifting a row of length n by n positions results in the same row, we only care about k(modn).
Directional Neutrality: Whether we shift an element to the left or to the right, for the matrix to remain "similar," the element at index j must be equal to the element at the position it would be replaced by.
In a cyclic shift of k, the element at mat[i][j] must match mat[i][(j + k) % n].
Iteration: We iterate through every cell in the matrix. If any cell mat[i][j] does not match its counterpart at the shifted index, the matrix is not similar.

Here's the Code:

class Solution {
    public boolean areSimilar(int[][] mat, int k) {
        int m = mat.length;
        int n = mat[0].length;
        k %= n;
        
        if (k == 0) return true;

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (mat[i][j] != mat[i][(j + k) % n]) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }
        
        return true;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(m×n)
Space Complexity: O(1)


